1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,160
Imagine writing a New Year's resolution, right?

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:06,640
You put it in a drawer and, uh, just expect your family to magically follow it.

3
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:08,460
Yeah, that never works out.

4
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:15,080
Exactly. But that is, like, exactly what happens when a government team writes a digital roadmap in total isolation.

5
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:20,360
It looks great on paper, but without a real system of accountability, it binds absolutely no one.

6
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:23,680
Zero accountability. Right. So welcome to this deep dive.

7
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:29,800
Today, we are exploring the complex mechanics of getting massive digital roadmaps officially adopted.

8
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:36,980
Whether you are, you know, managing projects in your ministry, or you are just fascinated by how complex systems get built,

9
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:41,480
this process is how you prevent systems from becoming fragmented and siloed.

10
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:44,500
And that New Year's analogy really hits the mark, honestly.

11
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:52,600
A roadmap only becomes the government's own when the bodies it actually binds have read it, and, you know, had their comments answered in writing,

12
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:55,260
and an authority has formally adopted it.

13
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:57,480
Which is a whole process in itself.

14
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:03,100
Oh, absolutely. To see how this works, we can look at the fictional country of Progresa as our model.

15
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:06,480
They use this very deliberate six-month blueprint.

16
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:09,540
So month one focuses entirely on consultation.

17
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:11,060
Okay, getting everyone's input.

18
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:18,480
Right. Then month two reconciles current initiatives and sets priorities, and month three is just a dedicated drafting sprint.

19
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:24,280
Wait, I have to ask, though: how do you prevent months one and two from turning into an endless listening tour?

20
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:27,020
Like, where everyone just airs their grievances and nothing gets done.

21
00:01:27,380 --> 00:01:34,720
Yeah, that is the danger. Yeah. But you prevent that by structuring month two specifically around reconciling initiatives that are already underway.

22
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:38,620
You have to avoid building duplicated or rekeyed systems.

23
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:41,020
Ah. So you find the overlaps early.

24
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:48,800
Exactly. You set firm priorities on a gap register, which is basically a list of what is missing, before any actual drafting begins in month three.

25
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:56,800
Okay, so if prioritizing prevents duplication, how do you handle the inevitable collision when you present the draft to these different departments?

26
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:59,820
Like, I am guessing this isn't just a suggestion box.

27
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:00,980
No, definitely not.

28
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:05,080
You need a formalized way to say yes or no without, uh, starting a turf war.

29
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:07,680
Right, and that is the function of months four and five.

30
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:10,160
They utilize something called the response matrix.

31
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:15,920
Uh, departments submit written comments, and in the matrix, you record the comment, the role of the commenter-

32
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:18,460
Wait, just the role. Right. Not the name.

33
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:21,160
Never the specific name, keeping it objective.

34
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:26,580
You record the role, your response, the decision, and the exact change made.

35
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:29,440
The decisions fall into three distinct categories:

36
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:30,560
accepted,

37
00:02:31,220 --> 00:02:33,700
accepted in part, or not accepted.

38
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:37,080
It sounds like a heavily structured receipt system.

39
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:41,420
You are keeping the receipts for every piece of feedback, so nobody can say they were ignored.

40
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:43,940
That is precisely what it is.

41
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:51,560
For example, in Progresa, the finance ministry asked for the mathematical basis behind a high cost estimate for a learner register.

42
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,180
The drafting team provided it and kept the estimate range.

43
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:57,320
So that would be accepted in part.

44
00:02:57,760 --> 00:02:58,380
Exactly.

45
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:04,480
But when the examination authority wanted to move their registration project up to the first horizon, meaning phase one,

46
00:03:05,180 --> 00:03:06,280
that was not accepted.

47
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:09,320
Because phase one needs to establish the foundation first.

48
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:09,900
Correct.

49
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:15,040
The response noted that moving them up depended on the learner register covering every district first.

50
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:20,860
Answering every comment in writing creates a binding consensus, so everyone feels heard,

51
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:23,200
even if their specific idea is rejected.

52
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:27,620
And this feedback phase can actually alter the raw evidence itself, too.

53
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:32,880
I saw in the reference architecture notes that a government voluntarily lowered its own score.

54
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:36,520
Yeah, which is surprising but crucial for accuracy.

55
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:39,260
The digital government authority in Progresa

56
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:44,280
actually pointed out that an interoperability score was way too high.

57
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:51,520
Wow. Really? Yeah. The original draft based the score on a, a desk result, basically theoretical paperwork,

58
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:54,600
rather than a verified real-world position.

59
00:03:55,560 --> 00:04:00,000
Because of that feedback, the score was lowered from two point five to one point five.

60
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:04,560
That is wild. It is like asking a teacher to lower your A to a C.

61
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:12,620
But I guess sacrificing short-term optics prevents you from designing systems based on false premises, which would be, you know, incredibly costly to change later.

62
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:20,220
Exactly. And the change was recorded right beside the maturity table, so any reader could see exactly why it moved.

63
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:26,980
Okay, so we have prevented duplication, we have managed egos with the matrix, and reality-checked our data.

64
00:04:27,660 --> 00:04:30,900
How does this document actually get stamped and adopted in month six?

65
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:39,560
Month six is the final polish. Drafting assistants systematically prepare the response matrix, revise the roadmap and investment case,

66
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:41,760
and run final consistency checks.

67
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:47,820
The UNDP playbook actually rates stakeholder engagement extremely high during this specific step.

68
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:50,360
Because it proves everyone was involved.

69
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:50,940
Right.

70
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:53,340
The goal isn't just a pristine document.

71
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:57,000
It is a document everyone has already debated and agreed upon.

72
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:05,300
The reference architecture ends its entire procedure using this exact logic, finalizing with an action plan, monitoring, and continuous improvement.

73
00:05:05,960 --> 00:05:09,780
You are essentially baking the buy-in into the drafting process itself.

74
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:10,500
Exactly.

75
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:18,900
So bringing it all together, a digital roadmap only becomes reality when every single body comments, every comment is answered in writing,

76
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:25,280
and an authority officially adopts it. That is how it moves from a fragile wish list to a binding commitment.

77
00:05:25,820 --> 00:05:27,700
It truly becomes the government's own.

